WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
llama.

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:09.119
cpp is a low-level local inference ecosystem that supports compatible model formats and multiple hardware backends.

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:17.554
It offers detailed control and can serve as a foundation for command-line tools, applications, and local servers.

00:00:17.554 --> 00:00:22.798
Ollama emphasizes straightforward model management and a local service interface.

00:00:22.798 --> 00:00:29.865
LM Studio provides a desktop experience for discovering, loading, testing, and serving supported models.

00:00:29.865 --> 00:00:34.653
Both can simplify common workflows while retaining runtime-specific limits.

00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:42.632
Open WebUI provides a browser-based interface that can connect to supported local or remote model services.

00:00:42.632 --> 00:00:47.875
Text-generation-webui offers a broader experimental interface at a high level.

00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:53.119
More features also create more dependencies, settings, and security surface.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:58.818
Local APIs allow scripts and applications to call a model service.

00:00:58.818 --> 00:01:09.989
Compatibility with a familiar API shape can ease integration, but authentication, network binding, request limits, logging, and data exposure still require deliberate configuration.

00:01:09.989 --> 00:01:19.336
Model management includes source verification, license review, naming, storage, checksums where appropriate, prompt templates, version records, evaluation, and retirement.

00:01:19.336 --> 00:01:25.492
A model file is a software and supply-chain dependency, not just content.

00:01:25.492 --> 00:01:30.279
Start with the simplest tool that meets the requirement.

00:01:30.279 --> 00:01:38.258
Validate one model and workflow before adding orchestration, agents, retrieval, multiple users, routing, or distributed services.

00:01:38.258 --> 00:01:41.222
Complexity should follow measured need.
